1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,330
Contractors do the work.

2
00:00:02,410 --> 00:00:04,670
Then they wait to get paid.

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:09,720
In Rabbet's 2024 survey, 82% of US contractors waited more than 30 days for payment.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,500
In 2025, 88% declined to bid for clients with a slow-pay reputation.

5
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:17,670
Slow payments cost US construction an estimated 299 billion dollars in 2025.

6
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:21,070
Payment is slow because proof is slow: photos, invoices,

7
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:23,670
and someone checking them before any money moves.

8
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:25,750
Take one leak.

9
00:00:25,830 --> 00:00:28,170
The kitchen sink in unit 4B.

10
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:33,170
Punchlist Pay pays the contractor the moment the work is proven.

11
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:37,100
The manager types the request in plain English.

12
00:00:37,180 --> 00:00:41,630
Claude turns it into a work order: the unit, a budget, a deadline,

13
00:00:41,710 --> 00:00:46,280
and three criteria that a photo, a receipt or a timestamp can prove.

14
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:50,060
Then two Claude agents negotiate.

15
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:52,910
Ours works for the manager.

16
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:56,270
Mike's agent knows his real rates.

17
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:58,530
Ours opens at $220.

18
00:00:58,610 --> 00:01:02,910
Mike's agent counters at $245, and ours accepts.

19
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:08,310
Our agent then creates a PayPal order for exactly that amount.

20
00:01:08,890 --> 00:01:12,180
The manager approves it in PayPal.

21
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:15,490
The money is authorized and held.

22
00:01:15,570 --> 00:01:18,640
Not captured, and not yet paid.

23
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:23,830
On site, Mike checks in with a photo.

24
00:01:23,910 --> 00:01:30,630
That releases the 20% deposit, $49, through PayPal Payouts.

25
00:01:31,210 --> 00:01:35,680
When he's done, he uploads the after photo and the receipt.

26
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:41,770
Claude's Verifier checks each criterion against the images and quotes what it sees.

27
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:44,550
It reads the receipt and sees dry pipes.

28
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:46,990
But these sample photos carry no timestamps,

29
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:50,100
and it can't confirm it's the same cabinet as the check-in.

30
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:51,630
So nothing pays itself.

31
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:55,870
The rules in code hold the balance and send the job to the manager with the evidence.

32
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,800
The manager approves it.

33
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:03,180
That override is logged next to the original verdict.

34
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:09,380
The authorization is captured, and the $196 balance goes out as a PayPal payout,

35
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:11,540
confirmed by PayPal.

36
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:14,100
Mike sees it on his phone.

37
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:17,000
Here's a job that failed.

38
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:21,470
A cleaner sent a photo of unit 4C, taken before check-in.

39
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:23,870
The Verifier caught both.

40
00:02:23,950 --> 00:02:28,090
Nothing was paid, and the manager can request a redo.

41
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:30,780
The ledger is an AG Grid.

42
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:35,690
Every row carries its proof, the verdict, and how fast the money moved,

43
00:02:35,770 --> 00:02:39,240
with live totals of what's held and what's paid.

44
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:42,180
PayPal Orders, Payments and Payouts move the money.

45
00:02:42,260 --> 00:02:44,160
Claude scopes, negotiates and verifies.

46
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:47,610
The ledger is AG Grid, and the whole app runs on Render with Render Postgres.

47
00:02:47,690 --> 00:02:49,740
Hard rules in code decide when money moves.

48
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:51,340
Punchlist Pay.

49
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:52,820
Promised, proven, paid.

50
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:55,240
Try it at punchlist-pay dot onrender dot com.
